  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 205 PM CDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nMonday through Saturday:\n\nSynopsis: A elongated area of upper troughing will progress into \ncentral portions of the continent Monday through the middle of the \nweek, slowly dragging an attendant surface front eastward in the \nprocess. The trough and surface front will pull close enough to \ninfluence southern Wisconsin's weather Monday afternoon into at \nleast Tuesday night, when widespread rain and a few thunderstorms \nare possible. Precise pattern evolution becomes considerably more \nuncertain from mid-week onward, with a multitude of potential \nsolutions regarding the progression & fate of the early week trough \nand surface front in-play. With some potential for one or both of \nthe features to linger in the vicinity of southern Wisconsin, \nconditional periods of rain chances continue through the end of next \nweek. Temperatures are expected to remain either at or above early \nNovember normals through the duration of the period.  \n\nMonday afternoon through Tuesday: The most confident window of the \nlong term forecast period opens as a coupling of low & mid-upper \nsynoptic forcing arrive into the area, driving widespread rain \npotential. Expecting precip to begin Monday afternoon & evening as \nlarge scale warm advection increases regionally, with rain potential \nextending into Tuesday as the primary surface front & upper wave \nencroach from the west. Available medium-range forecast soundings \nhint at some weak MUCAPE through this portion of the period, so \ncan't entirely rule out a few thunderstorms mixing in with the \nwidespread rainfall. Tend to think that projected MUCAPE values will \nbe too meager to support any stronger thunderstorms, but will be \nmonitoring trends. Presence of embedded convection within an \nenvironment characterized by 1.3\"+ precipitable water values could, \nhowever, lead to some brief heavy downpours in storms. Not \nanticipating a widespread flooding threat at this time given the \nisolated & brief nature of any such downpours. Initial probabilities \nfrom the NBM point toward 0.25-0.50\" rainfall totals areawide, with \nthe potential for locally higher totals to 0.75\"+ where any heavier \ndownpours focus.  \n\nWednesday-Friday: Off/on, conditional rain chances remain forecast \nwith some potential for the early week surface front and/or upper \ntrough to linger regionally. Whether one/both of these features \nactually do linger will depend on 1) the phasing of northern and \nsouthern perturbations embedded within the broader area of longwave \ntroughing and 2) the subsequent impacts on the strength of the \nsurface front. If upper phasing occurs, height falls will linger \nover the western Great Lakes through the end of the week, combining \nwith the remains of the surface front to support continued off/on \nrain chances. If phasing doesn't occur, a tendency toward height \nrises will become favored, combining with a decaying surface front \nto support drier conditions. Neither deterministic nor ensemble-\nbased guidance point toward a favored solution at the moment, with \ntrends being monitored for convergence over coming updates. Given \nthe prevailing uncertainty, have maintained precip probabilities \nfrom the NBM in the afternoon update & will plan to refine as this \nportion of the period draws closer. \n\nQuigley",
